window.docContents[146].resolve({"/Distance/#section-diameter-and-radius-of-the-hypercube":{"contents":"Diameters add under box products (ediam_boxProd) and the complete graph on Bool\nhas diameter 1 (ediam_top), so by induction the diameter of Q[n] is n. The\nbase case is the one-vertex graph Q[0], whose diameter is 0\n(ediam_eq_zero_of_subsingleton); we gave HypercubeVert 0 a Subsingleton\ninstance back in Computations With Hypercubes.\n\nlemma ediam_hypercube {n : ℕ} : Q[n].ediam = n := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => exact ediam_eq_zero_of_subsingleton\n  | succ n ih => rw [hypercube_succ, ediam_boxProd, ih, ediam_top, Nat.cast_succ]\n\n\nlemma diam_hypercube {n : ℕ} : Q[n].diam = n := by\n  rw [SimpleGraph.diam, ediam_hypercube, ENat.toNat_natCast]\n\n\nSince the hypercube is vertex-transitive (isVertexTransitive_hypercube) its radius\nequals its diameter, and every vertex is central.\n\nlemma radius_hypercube {n : ℕ} : Q[n].radius = n := by\n  rw [(isVertexTransitive_hypercube n).radius_eq_ediam, ediam_hypercube]\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Eccentricity, Diameter and Radius","header":"11.2.3. Diameter and Radius of the Hypercube","id":"/Distance/#section-diameter-and-radius-of-the-hypercube"},"/Theorems/#section-tur-n-graphs":{"contents":"The extremal graphs are the Turán graphs. Mathlib defines turanGraph n r on\nFin n, with v and w adjacent iff they have different residues modulo r.\n\nThe Turán graph T(9, 3)\n\n#source turanGraph\n\n\nThe residue classes modulo r are the parts, and two vertices are adjacent iff they\nlie in different parts, so this is a complete r-partite graph. Its parts are\nbalanced: the residue classes of 0, …, n - 1 modulo r have sizes ⌊n / r⌋ or\n⌈n / r⌉. For example, when r divides n the Turán graph turanGraph n r is\nisomorphic to Mathlib's completeEquipartiteGraph r (n / r), which has r parts of\nsize n / r.\n\n#check completeEquipartiteGraph.turanGraph\n-- completeEquipartiteGraph r t ≃g turanGraph (r * t) r\n\n\nThe case r = 0 is a junk value. Since v % 0 = v in Lean, any two distinct\nvertices are adjacent, so turanGraph n 0 = ⊤. This graph has edges, but there is no\nsuch thing as a complete 0-partite graph on a nonempty vertex set. So results\nabout Turán graphs usually assume 0 < r.\n\n#check turanGraph_zero -- turanGraph n 0 = ⊤\n\n\n#check turanGraph_cliqueFree -- 0 < r → (turanGraph n r).CliqueFree (r + 1)\n\n\nThe number of edges of turanGraph n r is called turanNumber n r. Turán graphs\nhave decidable adjacency, so we can compute it.\n\n#eval turanNumber 10 3 -- 33\n\n\n#check turanNumber_eq\n-- turanNumber n r = (n ^ 2 - (n % r) ^ 2) * (r - 1) / (2 * r) + (n % r).choose 2\n\n\nIn particular turanNumber n r is at most (1 - 1/r) n²/2.\n\n#check mul_turanNumber_le -- 2 * r * turanNumber n r ≤ (r - 1) * n ^ 2\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Extremal Graph Theory","header":"13.2.1. Turán Graphs","id":"/Theorems/#section-tur-n-graphs"}});